[1] In 1816, the French physician René Laennec faced an awkward problem. [2] A young patient had come to him with symptoms of heart disease, and the usual way to listen to a heart was for the doctor to press an ear directly against the patient's chest. [3] {{1|Laennec, who considered this method both unreliable and improper for this patient, looked for another way.}}

[4] He remembered seeing children play near a pile of wooden beams. [5] One child scratched the end of a beam with a pin, {{2|and the other child, listening at the far end, could hear}} the faint sound clearly. [6] Laennec rolled a sheet of paper into a tight tube, placed one end on the patient's chest, and put his ear to the other. [7] The heartbeat came through {{3|louder and clearer then}} it ever had with his ear alone.

[8] Over the next several years, Laennec {{4|experimented with tubes of different woods and lengths, and eventually settled}} on a hollow wooden cylinder about a foot long. [9] He called the instrument a *stethoscope*, from Greek words meaning "chest" and "to examine." [10] More important than the device {{5|itself, however was}} the careful method he developed for using it. [11] He listened to hundreds of patients, recorded the sounds he heard, and later compared {{6|them}} with what doctors found when they examined the patients' bodies after death.

[12] {{7|Laennec described his findings in a book published in 1819.}} [13] In it, he named and described sounds that doctors still learn to recognize, linking each to a particular disease of the heart or lungs. [14] Not every physician welcomed the {{8|change. Some}} complained that the stethoscope put a tube between the doctor and the patient. [15] Within a few decades, however, the instrument had become so common that {{9|they were}} a symbol of the medical profession itself.

[16] Today's stethoscopes, with two flexible tubes and a pair of earpieces, look little like Laennec's wooden cylinder. [17] {{10|Yet doctors who use them are still doing what he did in 1816: listening carefully to the body.}}

[1] Wilson Bentley grew up on a farm in Jericho, Vermont, where winters were long and snow fell for months at a time. [2] When he was fifteen, his mother gave him an old microscope, and he spent the next winter peering at snowflakes. [3] {{1|The crystals melted, however, before he could draw them, so he could never finish a sketch.}}

[4] Bentley decided that a camera might succeed where his pencil had failed. [5] He persuaded his parents to buy a bellows camera{{2|, which}} he attached to the microscope. [6] {{3|Working outdoors in an unheated shed, his breath had to be held}} so that its warmth would not melt the crystal on the slide. [7] He handled each flake with a feather {{4|and also moved it carefully with a thin wooden splint, both tools}} that would not melt the ice. [8] On January 15, 1885, after many failed attempts, he made the first known photograph of a single snow crystal.

[9] Over the next forty-six years, Bentley photographed more than five thousand snowflakes. [10] Some of {{5|his neighbors, who}} considered the project a waste of time, laughed at the farmer who stood in storms with a camera. [11] Scientists, {{6|however found}} his images valuable. [12] The photographs showed that each crystal's six arms grew in step with one another, a pattern that later researchers linked to the temperature and humidity the crystal passed through as it fell. {{7|}}

[13] In 1931, Bentley and the meteorologist William Humphreys published *Snow Crystals*, a book of more than two thousand of {{8|Bentley's}} images. [14] The collection of photographs that filled its pages {{9|were}} soon used by designers of textiles and jewelry as well as by scientists. [15] That December, Bentley walked six miles home through a blizzard, developed pneumonia, and died.

[16] {{10|Today, the shed where Bentley worked no longer stands, but the Jericho Historical Society keeps a collection of his glass-plate negatives.}} [17] Visitors can see the {{11|negatives which}} still preserve flakes that melted more than a century ago.
